#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b322d9c-37ab-52f7-9c7e-158b2aad5a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336727} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa8641b-f12e-5282-8639-b287c9ca081d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x-xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336764} {:answer/id #uuid "778b8f44-4e6f-5e84-b5d0-3856a65b2fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337107} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0a7e37-7922-5237-be4e-0d4daf2edd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x-2y}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337313} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ffdd28-a653-5368-a470-1763333eed55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x+2xy}{{x}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b322d9c-37ab-52f7-9c7e-158b2aad5a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336727} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ea9a9d-1024-53db-ae2f-47b539ab86b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b84d5b9-93df-56c4-be18-4346c8b72764" :question/math-academy-id "q-72260" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y+4y=2{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+4y & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x]+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4) & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a712bc-5c5d-5646-9703-82e77d69b46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403552} {:answer/id #uuid "c63beba4-7686-58ae-845d-111254d3e6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403553} {:answer/id #uuid "5996d30b-a352-5547-9b35-f09224ff8616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403554} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5db3c-106b-5a20-8512-215f226e23f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403555} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9e24e9-f8f1-50d2-a309-392b65ff2168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5db3c-106b-5a20-8512-215f226e23f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403555} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f6e4f21-8993-51e5-a1c9-737356240b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbca085f-0b60-5f9a-bdc1-1da6491170fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-7230" :question/problem "![](images/87/8764149ca0000522277d48bf48f4ef14ba06f7e4793ba1a58ef639ff7af3a3b9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={3}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={3}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={3}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$ Therefore, $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "760d4f2f-67c3-578b-90ce-e44b5cc0e475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403559} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ff124-ce1b-58ec-a4c4-728cb4b536df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403560} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb95f56-7efa-56a4-ab5b-5f4220d4b140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403561} {:answer/id #uuid "840799e0-019f-5e56-960e-e4dce9f6a43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403562} {:answer/id #uuid "97c4002b-68c4-5b92-bb46-3a3c52c7bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bb95f56-7efa-56a4-ab5b-5f4220d4b140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403561} :answer-field/id #uuid "117f36b7-d2dd-58cf-81f2-744857f29ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2d08ab-7e1f-579d-8314-e037ebc00133" :question/math-academy-id "q-7232" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ad72b3df34f32d6a76f79f6801887bd6fe1d762fefd74071517a754b1890ac2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={2}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={2}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2905b3c7-5120-5928-8659-ab7cf49215cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336415} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9f02de-6945-5986-9bd6-77c4fda53a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337447} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c17bce-f321-5a5a-b8a2-e2f57aa0f0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337781} {:answer/id #uuid "d1424795-2c4b-569b-984c-33e713fb9a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337866} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baaf2a-856c-5b97-ba7e-56d8806079c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3baaf2a-856c-5b97-ba7e-56d8806079c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338162} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d342821-bbce-5fd1-b3bb-822b8446cf81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83ace1c9-0a10-59aa-bb99-7a86a6dbfa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-72333" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{1/x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd6dd70-5f78-54b6-ac97-950f2f384aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336473} {:answer/id #uuid "4e08260e-b4f6-5c7a-ad2d-8294f523a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336757} {:answer/id #uuid "5b538add-4209-5721-8b56-652729cc391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336862} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7564a4-f738-5faf-a2c3-bdd625a0ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337163} {:answer/id #uuid "bb83b45c-700e-5a49-955e-7ea35708e865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e08260e-b4f6-5c7a-ad2d-8294f523a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336757} :answer-field/id #uuid "64250102-7382-5959-9fea-4e5f4501809d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0dae206-0030-50d7-a470-3e9d541986a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72336" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c029288-0577-53f2-b622-20d182eb4958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336446} {:answer/id #uuid "537b5f65-839c-5501-a32d-7c7e7a49a12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336806} {:answer/id #uuid "77455b1e-3f23-5bec-b73a-1b6c9dc86ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337106} {:answer/id #uuid "9e26fb10-c993-50f5-8b93-429253440f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337463} {:answer/id #uuid "e2592f07-77fc-5726-895f-b9958ca63d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2592f07-77fc-5726-895f-b9958ca63d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338000} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f9d37f-e6cb-53f6-b39d-a73c1eb6742f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a9948ce-563f-5b0f-bd31-1937130137d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72339" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181818f9-ee5d-570b-8805-3453bec6da26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336257} {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} {:answer/id #uuid "601c5f25-3385-59f3-b9ec-6f4783410687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336901} {:answer/id #uuid "d13e9d9a-a682-5a93-b274-9657dd12c702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337864} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d6ab9a-d942-55d2-8674-766bbfa15318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} :answer-field/id #uuid "1287170e-aae1-5d7e-9ef3-ec229bb6713f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94a2c36c-7f20-5b89-950f-742f856d9127" :question/math-academy-id "q-72382" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $5{x}^{4}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the radical. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{1/2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{u}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{5}$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b16a2-0458-5600-9a6f-f4f6e552fd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336202} {:answer/id #uuid "4c671da3-c302-5f3c-9ea5-2dc6dac2cc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336742} {:answer/id #uuid "6fed05ea-acde-5be8-b414-01135dc6f042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337023} {:answer/id #uuid "826ab4ce-caa8-5db7-9188-f1b3cd74a9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337218} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf95245-48b9-524a-917b-0ba71da5cdb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fed05ea-acde-5be8-b414-01135dc6f042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337023} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eab9c10-198f-58c5-918e-000cc777273e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f0333a8-0cc0-5b0c-9999-f5175286f364" :question/math-academy-id "q-7239" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1ca11413bbce2ff307e494fb5a79028535b76cff24619e6921620f88c3e274ec.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1,$ so $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k({2}^{0})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k(1)-1 \\\\ 2 & =k-1 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({2}^{x})-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49005b9e-8885-518c-a503-38932dbc77f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710} {:answer/id #uuid "a7777608-b4d4-5368-84cb-c7a407165b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337546} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7873cf-81b9-5aaf-8051-9f548d554ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337684} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad9d29-36d5-5b4e-bad4-d6181862fae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337768} {:answer/id #uuid "e97102d4-c948-5475-a8f4-3282b09cc04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49005b9e-8885-518c-a503-38932dbc77f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a06ece-0b13-5c0a-9459-301060485f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a92c523-6f37-5b9d-8ed7-2c5b4d7360ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-7241" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a78c4fc61bbf60e1cdfdba6303ecc1a9d5e09caa21d0879556106893a931a09.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,5.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=5.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k({2}^{0})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k+4 \\\\ 1.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=1.5({2}^{x})+4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2b47545-fdf1-5e6a-b0bb-bbbecb8067cc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6058cb14-4722-5ae2-982a-1d1d72759305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398963} {:answer/id #uuid "615ab3c3-1cc9-56db-a561-9ade7641ace4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398964} {:answer/id #uuid "2b200597-1f86-5ecd-b8bc-18b09c132aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398965} {:answer/id #uuid "13a3fa38-b420-5956-8a4b-2194f2d8ef30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398966} {:answer/id #uuid "5674f007-df38-5b04-ac90-1342c70182a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6058cb14-4722-5ae2-982a-1d1d72759305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398963} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee57767-a470-5ed2-888e-e7479cb9accb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c75bb9c-44e2-5f73-9a68-3c296ae237a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72424" :question/problem "$\\int 16(7-2x)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=7-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 16(7-2x)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 16{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\int {u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\int {u}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{8}+C \\\\  & =-(7-2x)^{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-(7-2x)^{8}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(7-2x)^{8}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-8(7-2x)^{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-2x)+0 \\\\  & =-8(7-2x)^{7}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =16(7-2x)^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea8c3ea-0518-506a-9945-ef3bf2bf7936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336171} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae31738-7e11-5b61-bda5-73b51a186dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336978} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd74f15-1558-5167-ae1a-0738b496e927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337459} {:answer/id #uuid "afeaa9dd-27fb-515f-9b9a-a9fc160293f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337612} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d07741-d286-59ef-b0bc-34120b2124b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea8c3ea-0518-506a-9945-ef3bf2bf7936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336171} :answer-field/id #uuid "14072edb-e48a-597d-a3df-afce4caaaa51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b16baf99-001c-5b72-bc61-2ffc6c75b449" :question/math-academy-id "q-72445" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=7-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|+C & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b76063-8fda-596f-8106-cbb57f2ef69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336093} {:answer/id #uuid "86f6a2e9-4d29-5e23-9617-09660280aaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337252} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ead48b-c59a-570e-a7de-39bcbc22b8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337638} {:answer/id #uuid "bec88b66-0b15-559a-8f7c-3341c51c2ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337729} {:answer/id #uuid "f6322442-f9d7-5f7c-ac2d-896d214f1436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec88b66-0b15-559a-8f7c-3341c51c2ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337729} :answer-field/id #uuid "209f233a-25a9-55a5-ab33-40122ec3bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d475585-492e-5142-bfa4-12e97136e061" :question/math-academy-id "q-72446" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5cd12b-daf8-57b4-a508-5b25997dfab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336323} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ccfc1-4a8e-5fe1-b80c-9d8713467d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2x+1}{2x-1}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336723} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0854d1-efd5-57d5-ad92-34e8d95cd84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336758} {:answer/id #uuid "89828476-cdcf-5b30-b125-067c9a0a1d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2x+1}{2x-1}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337271} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9493db-04ea-5567-b436-e98702a20a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2x+1}{2x-1}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f5cd12b-daf8-57b4-a508-5b25997dfab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336323} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd143701-6720-5620-9d38-200cfec896ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b89e488e-b744-5dec-bdcd-c1a1eb5f6a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72449" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(2x+1)(2x-1)|+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e59935f-f93b-599b-9816-d15f61104595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336167} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccce204-ca7a-5eee-bc02-3fcc7e16e632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336299} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5b5f53-8881-5e03-8cde-ddfcc41895a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336732} {:answer/id #uuid "75e7362c-a6a5-5145-bf5d-1527db0f317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337095} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc5cfb0-4b70-528a-a0e4-e88e561188b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e7362c-a6a5-5145-bf5d-1527db0f317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337095} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b5679a-f4f7-56bb-9b5b-253ee5665aec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de70203f-dd05-5f0d-9800-283717012618" :question/math-academy-id "q-72467" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the $x$ that's outside the square root isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{2}-5).$ But the derivative is $2x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc7ed7d-8822-50d6-adbe-ad3f4f94cc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336309} {:answer/id #uuid "2855b2b9-6a8b-51fc-bc86-192bc1d62f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336410} {:answer/id #uuid "62f307be-5f4b-5771-b9ee-77edd782e456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336922} {:answer/id #uuid "75294229-d638-5281-9b75-127f796a0330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337087} {:answer/id #uuid "a40c3d37-1f3e-56af-9259-897cf2fc43fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc7ed7d-8822-50d6-adbe-ad3f4f94cc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336309} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe47395-e6a4-5174-8bac-b9841d8af59f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ca56a13-e2a8-587b-a791-0b4526ed9f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7249" :question/problem "![](images/db/db4c6694a3bc172aea8a47f1a828eac8235286b7ce6b7e327574790a6c31c270.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad7f19f7-5593-5372-821e-d1252d3b8446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337587} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e66778-1d33-59c9-9c63-84193616625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337745} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4a56a8-6d0c-5363-9d42-b65beb462d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337846} {:answer/id #uuid "cf58d7b6-9e04-5b2a-b15f-7c5224686dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337847} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e1b841-fe74-5a6f-9da0-dd60389da801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e1b841-fe74-5a6f-9da0-dd60389da801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338222} :answer-field/id #uuid "833a989b-8bb1-589d-92ce-6ba9062090a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdd0bbdf-150a-52db-a038-e888ce1f0809" :question/math-academy-id "q-72524" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{x}{2}+7?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-\\frac{x}{2}+7$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=2x+19$ has a slope of $2.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=2x+19$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128902a2-01ba-574b-8cb8-9e24d91588d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403594} {:answer/id #uuid "d421b62c-91bc-51bd-9a1e-dcb674541fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403595} {:answer/id #uuid "89b95e28-4bdc-577f-9fef-39eb015deed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403596} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a6792-46dd-54a0-852a-c3ce900255d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403597} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf02042-68e8-57bc-821d-c4e709f6e6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a6792-46dd-54a0-852a-c3ce900255d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403597} :answer-field/id #uuid "86226cb6-da89-5070-bdaf-be3482566831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80543b3-4997-5175-82f2-660b5af23e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7253" :question/problem "![](images/50/50be1f4a894e86b5f06fba18655caca163c3d4c7a3423d2ecf159aa49975eb8c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1.$ Therefore, $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460f3e7e-a692-5e84-84d3-bb04895e42c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339750} {:answer/id #uuid "7de51bcc-dba4-5f66-9ed1-e89e2285349b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340264} {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1a70a6-b427-555b-b378-aaf49435f770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341084} {:answer/id #uuid "f252e1db-2d4f-5cfe-b195-696e6f606c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e67ea3-0bdd-589c-834c-8bb372afcf1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99fe5e6a-20b3-53d0-a013-c1c2c0d3ad0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72568" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1988b7-4bc8-5bda-86c5-639e0552dedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339683} {:answer/id #uuid "51dac7ff-2d97-5661-b543-ad769758a707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339880} {:answer/id #uuid "6d11b816-6f21-575c-a776-01785873cb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340089} {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} {:answer/id #uuid "af6755f9-b09b-562d-a589-5067ac2ec549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e7ea68-a247-56ce-a08d-252af6a5df70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad191a02-5856-5306-8980-ab9514c55d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72571" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-5}{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(5,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-5) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x+y & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}x+y) & =3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 2x+3y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424f1750-2255-5cd3-97f1-bff8ae1f779e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339710} {:answer/id #uuid "64a24bd8-02d1-5da1-875a-ff8313d79449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5(2-5{x}^{5})^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340018} {:answer/id #uuid "cd66158f-066f-573b-a023-86fcc42573cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2(2-5{x}^{5})^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340950} {:answer/id #uuid "d40a6f74-0d5f-5192-b1f2-24adfd672a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341003} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c87e78-2256-558f-a463-3af7ee7e05a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c87e78-2256-558f-a463-3af7ee7e05a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341259} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f33047-2f0b-50bf-84ac-e403b1fabf2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "958efc2b-d91b-53e5-b712-1690bc1b7068" :question/math-academy-id "q-72605" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2-5{x}^{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,(-\\frac{1}{25})\\text{d}u={x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\cdot {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{25})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3520d323-c6a4-5bce-bac1-1f4c02bd0068" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and a Point on the Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ceba2e-461c-5d6f-94d0-fffabcaa7f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339737} {:answer/id #uuid "67364410-21ff-5ab6-b499-984469e0f0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340042} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6b7a0c-3051-526a-9636-efd77cb0b591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340268} {:answer/id #uuid "c00a31e7-30ad-5958-a723-a2568cb7ceb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340844} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdecbe3-6add-5929-86dd-7b81307f2bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67364410-21ff-5ab6-b499-984469e0f0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340042} :answer-field/id #uuid "59370454-96d7-5c69-8869-e408beefd110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b538b794-53e2-55d6-941e-f772138f1dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72621" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e68ede9db2528ea607fc6fd63f0ec4ca1c1378982239913ef49153e1cf5b609.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(3,-3)$ and the point $X(-1,2)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(2-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{41})^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3332b534-bd04-5e86-995e-1e6d691d8e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733d9013-5828-5e8a-a6ca-98d0ee84db3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340151} {:answer/id #uuid "9e08cb06-fa94-559a-b45f-0247593ae993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340553} {:answer/id #uuid "ce427bad-7fbf-553d-94e7-4eac1f2a458e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340960} {:answer/id #uuid "d512d53f-d1ef-5fcf-a459-a5ecee4d6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341012} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb559b7-bf25-5a4d-8461-b1784d7b4dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d512d53f-d1ef-5fcf-a459-a5ecee4d6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341012} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c77d87a-34f1-5f64-8f04-06a22766d6be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1e203a-fce6-56f0-bd3a-5a969f3f611d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72711" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "868125f0-22e0-57fc-9620-f3242419a689" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Rational Expression Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d5cf9e-61a5-56dc-ab85-48cc3361b6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339348} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4a8bfb-24ab-5d4f-85c0-b03c7be1078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339502} {:answer/id #uuid "5af65e91-2dda-5a02-a6b9-84e591e12cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339956} {:answer/id #uuid "94adee08-1ae4-5711-87c2-0b92afcedb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340463} {:answer/id #uuid "a2128603-79a4-5ac2-aad8-d0e79ce06174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94adee08-1ae4-5711-87c2-0b92afcedb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340463} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ffbbed8-ad51-5238-b319-cf240d8126f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "508dcfe6-ec7a-5fa0-ab23-63e62cd88ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72712" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f07a6b5-32ea-529d-9160-238820145dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339267} {:answer/id #uuid "174b9032-2956-564a-826e-c2c81592003e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339336} {:answer/id #uuid "508acfdf-0761-5691-be12-cc8ecd53beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339862} {:answer/id #uuid "84059e1d-50ea-57c1-a02c-e73cba67744f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340317} {:answer/id #uuid "f79db4a6-d5f5-5ae3-9697-f4ab3f3df76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "508acfdf-0761-5691-be12-cc8ecd53beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339862} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1b3e9c-7be4-50dc-84b7-55a05c94254f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d86eb6a4-4303-50bf-b47a-fa898e3f8037" :question/math-academy-id "q-72764" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f53996727138b9442ac041cf492618db8287b54ba652c4f9e375cdde27dc1862.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $34{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =67.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =53.190… \\\\  & ≈53{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =53-34 \\\\  & =19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d011d15-855e-593b-a305-503c6f4d7b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339829} {:answer/id #uuid "5049855a-0cf1-52ff-a6d7-82ad580a1998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339858} {:answer/id #uuid "65970e9b-6b55-55f9-87cb-642a2bfcf59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340026} {:answer/id #uuid "810e57dc-6211-51e2-9530-7c30c274a5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340286} {:answer/id #uuid "941f7586-f901-520c-ba5a-d3f77fa4895c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5049855a-0cf1-52ff-a6d7-82ad580a1998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339858} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a7e60a-6a2a-50ed-b8ee-1ebc1c309e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1e965d5-3d0b-5c28-9671-3eb1e1598d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72768" :question/problem "![](images/36/36af7324fd2112d94feddc5a1a02a91d97bbc0866cb93a6ca711dd73f566305a.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$" :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7c/7cfcb5cdf77b4afbf25afd64e37bb33f0ab2c211e09659181bfc16db0ccd2db7.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈67.658,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 67.658 \\\\  & =135.316.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $135.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bedf0ef-d091-529c-9452-055d4df48089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339962} {:answer/id #uuid "7b290d59-ce77-5ad9-b904-8c21b160ec07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340231} {:answer/id #uuid "907bfe3d-4729-5dfa-8728-e4a9413ce8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340423} {:answer/id #uuid "d409ed3f-53f8-5d27-b84d-7ce80fe544a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341002} {:answer/id #uuid "ea330265-17ea-5ce6-9db7-de0ab2bee739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bedf0ef-d091-529c-9452-055d4df48089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339962} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d12b9f-78ac-5e61-9bc9-9638586d0302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e89bb93-f1e6-5d54-9eb6-f01d87f935ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72914" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-4x-2(0)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062499b4-be47-5b28-982d-86c642bba935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339180} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9142-707f-5bcc-a580-ee524bee6fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339234} {:answer/id #uuid "359a8a35-2225-5f23-8b9d-aae7b68bb0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339604} {:answer/id #uuid "96928fb6-9fc9-5f9b-b89c-531b735c25b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340487} {:answer/id #uuid "c6587bc5-7cad-5be9-a21b-564e512cd0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96928fb6-9fc9-5f9b-b89c-531b735c25b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340487} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f07086c-8b55-5351-9a76-5d3e1c547bcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "241c4369-248c-546e-84df-6e2b47d7a0e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72998" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6d5146afb9508532145180a4f632c0b5c4495487b0a9a89cfd4f71e134b95f50.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-1.5).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(-1.5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-1.5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-2,1].$ Notice that $x=-1.5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/22/2216d3014a8c74102d5e298c300092a575798de0b66d89914716898bc16ac655.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-2}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1.5)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e3a595-a9ae-5d8c-9aa5-c8e3403fcb28" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae24c67-169d-568f-9906-74f5b3cc2e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339505} {:answer/id #uuid "35c068a3-dbe1-516f-a719-8c52f792f017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{n}^{2}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339606} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc01694-bcd6-5105-860b-e7ae3171d223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339824} {:answer/id #uuid "9d31eea3-a5b0-5f91-81ac-06d7e2e82a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-1)(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340545} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8da782-b040-553f-a209-d1c9c3875b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ae24c67-169d-568f-9906-74f5b3cc2e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339505} :answer-field/id #uuid "20784676-1346-5beb-bf37-2d8560fbcb5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81d120ad-3169-5d64-9a97-a76dfeab46ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-73023" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!}{(n-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot \\cancel{(n-1)!}}{\\cancel{(n-1)!}} \\\\  & =n(n+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e3a595-a9ae-5d8c-9aa5-c8e3403fcb28" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29487ed3-a525-52dd-9167-d626037dc815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339491} {:answer/id #uuid "7f774ed2-5f28-52ae-865d-32a61cabb3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340273} {:answer/id #uuid "9c213996-40cb-543e-9a55-403ef758636c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340533} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2f4e0c-cc16-5245-84c3-7f919dd4a20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340543} {:answer/id #uuid "dca1f91a-c517-5f9d-abf6-091011b8edcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29487ed3-a525-52dd-9167-d626037dc815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339491} :answer-field/id #uuid "247d41fd-6e73-5f0c-95b3-a198cf2975b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72330c15-2550-5193-88bf-8afccb0b16d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73028" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge -1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+2)!=(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n+1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(n+1)!}}{(n+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "644b7f09-7f66-50f2-8eba-ef6e009429d0" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04802008-4ed6-5b73-83f5-d29dc06587f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339160} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba86355-0ac6-5962-ae05-e44d9250bd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339366} {:answer/id #uuid "af54b886-24b1-56d3-9d8e-4c71dd5f011f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340727} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bcde05-f467-51e7-b960-da690faabda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k}{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341287} {:answer/id #uuid "f7792b0b-5d92-53fc-957c-3f59a201b930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+2k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af54b886-24b1-56d3-9d8e-4c71dd5f011f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340727} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83b7853-94c1-56fb-bb60-7f7706a1acaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c27af90-5244-552c-a6c5-2d976ec6599f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73037" :question/problem "Given that $k\\ge -1$ is an integer, $\\frac{(k+2)((k+1)!)^{2}}{((k+2)!)^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)(k+1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $k!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(k+2)((k+1)!)^{2}}{((k+2)!)^{2}} & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\cdot (k+1)!}{(k+2)!\\cdot (k+2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\cdot (k+1)!}{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(k+2)}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}}{(k+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+2)}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "143766e0-0ef5-5caf-b699-d394d4a707e9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d39e1d2-809f-5155-a07c-2be82fe6f5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339382} {:answer/id #uuid "40729786-50b6-5ff5-b256-a96bf88930f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339704} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3fcfc4-5970-535a-9851-ae25c946b062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{14})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340688} {:answer/id #uuid "dc526537-6440-5beb-89ba-a669341bd361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341086} {:answer/id #uuid "f54d10f1-3bdb-508e-83f2-6340ec8fe28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc526537-6440-5beb-89ba-a669341bd361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341086} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bdfe7ee-6a1b-5a6c-8716-9c970e741c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "188c3216-6730-58aa-bfa7-8985bab845cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-7305" :question/problem "Express $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{7})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ab5c9a-741e-507f-8a99-07a9f7aa451c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339588} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf55990-23dc-5693-8dde-0dd252a432b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339663} {:answer/id #uuid "48fe273f-13eb-5f78-8272-5b9b3a2db061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339782} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2895ac-425c-5001-bb2a-284e8601acaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339796} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ab1df-0cb6-5919-a2be-e9cca71bcc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a2895ac-425c-5001-bb2a-284e8601acaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339796} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf84d3cb-0dfa-513a-bcdd-3183ad8dee6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79464211-37d1-5cac-b164-284bb9d9308a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73105" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad3fb8a0cce2a6dac706d7876aca0a2339fe49c05cb7aa6ae54de86735ceafab.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=3$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=3$\n- The function is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=3,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=3,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce3626c4-68fc-5111-92bb-c6a72ef04b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[ln⁡(e+\\frac{4ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4e}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393252} {:answer/id #uuid "51976397-23e1-57d8-a039-644bf6317f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{(-2i/n)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393253} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2df163-f8cb-55a5-aa92-94fe12f6f8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[ln⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393254} {:answer/id #uuid "680c2096-a3a6-56c7-af41-ad8e3a2a9b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{(2i/n)}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393255} {:answer/id #uuid "7e48b7d1-54ea-5e38-b291-212dbf407565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[ln⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d2df163-f8cb-55a5-aa92-94fe12f6f8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[ln⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393254} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6ae5ee-b93f-55d8-b530-ad34a29b014a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2f3eda7-3ae2-5c51-adcd-171934d8313e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73156" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{e}^{3e}ln⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{3e}ln⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=ln⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=e,$ and the upper bound is $b=3e.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3e-e}{n}=\\frac{2e}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{3e}ln⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+i\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[ln⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516433a3-ddcf-5286-911f-d1661221641b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(\\frac{5k}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393266} {:answer/id #uuid "91a60e1f-ff52-5d12-8845-6de6b6f23736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393267} {:answer/id #uuid "6006a006-5f33-5ef4-b609-573993a53f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393268} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff05667-12f6-5f44-9206-bc6cf51be751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-{(-1+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393269} {:answer/id #uuid "af9dfa31-4950-5539-b718-f5c060dd0d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2(5+\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91a60e1f-ff52-5d12-8845-6de6b6f23736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393267} :answer-field/id #uuid "97365ecd-a18f-5653-861f-4053aa6db95b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5568099-5914-5fb0-903c-d4010e585001" :question/math-academy-id "q-73180" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=-2$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0d98b82-24d2-5b2c-b596-0300778bd187" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Two-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df5097ab-aeed-5dd2-a4ac-84a7912ba470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A=1$ and $B=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393280} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c9cc94-3908-54a8-9499-857e6df7b5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A=4$ and $B=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393281} {:answer/id #uuid "a5117579-9f3d-5b5b-bd73-6a66279018b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A=2$ and $B=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393282} {:answer/id #uuid "d57fb7dd-ef0f-5819-8fc5-009e2372e7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A=2$ and $B=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393283} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc0b9fc-a2e6-5d8a-9c55-1ce7d90a1edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A=-2$ and $B=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d57fb7dd-ef0f-5819-8fc5-009e2372e7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A=2$ and $B=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393283} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e286ea8-8e5b-5a6b-a9dc-7462355f83f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e5d3142-b826-521f-9a5c-2fb700156ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73189" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+1}{(x+1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the real constants $A$ and $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+1 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ -1 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+1 & =A(0+1)-1 \\\\ 2 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=2$ and $B=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f247351-8c81-595e-bb30-26630159819c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Missing Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9382f0-7a0d-5fdf-b6b4-fcd28ebbe39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339217} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3d14e6-bba3-5ffb-918b-631b55a400f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339650} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9bdf12-ba46-5ab6-bd2b-5d7ddb245855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339653} {:answer/id #uuid "5755ea0e-2f23-5d0e-bce6-ca54b7a0aa7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339927} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7fad86-5186-5452-a484-01b2b17a50d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a9bdf12-ba46-5ab6-bd2b-5d7ddb245855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339653} :answer-field/id #uuid "268d95a7-d6ca-5d10-b5ce-e2cd1c539891" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3df5e280-10bc-5c40-be58-baa686dafca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73195" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-2}{(x+3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+3}-\\frac{17}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+3)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2 & =A(x+3)-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)-2 & =A(0+3)-17 \\\\ -2 & =3A-17 \\\\ 15 & =3A \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e9c4ef2-0491-5b42-8679-4fe7dfbd7b00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Fraction Decomposition With Higher Order Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047cccdf-27f1-5cfb-b786-1c8d86f2746c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339159} {:answer/id #uuid "49e879e3-cea2-5684-be86-6af3707f8d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339792} {:answer/id #uuid "8aaa2710-0d0f-52e4-8149-15fbc048e9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340367} {:answer/id #uuid "91914e32-168a-51fa-af34-cb11e5d0549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340434} {:answer/id #uuid "d67f00c6-3a76-5e96-adcd-17772f5a5452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91914e32-168a-51fa-af34-cb11e5d0549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340434} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bffc3a-26ca-54b9-a2e5-c7f8ef639d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fc41a67-28eb-5a31-90bd-82ff488b2606" :question/math-academy-id "q-73384" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{10-{x}^{2}}{(x-4)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-4)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{x}^{2} & =A(x-4)^{2}+B(x-4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{4}^{2} & =A(4-4)^{2}+B(4-4)+C \\\\ -6 & =A(0)+B(0)+C \\\\ C & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{0}^{2} & =A(0-4)^{2}+B(0-4)-6 \\\\ 10 & =16A-4B-6 \\\\ 0 & =16A-4B-16 \\\\ 0 & =4A-B-4 \\\\ B & =4A-4.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=3$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{3}^{2} & =A(3-4)^{2}+B(3-4)-6 \\\\ 1 & =A-B-6 \\\\ 0 & =A-B-7 \\\\ B & =A-7.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A-4 & =A-7 \\\\ 3A & =-3 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $A$ into $(2)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =-1-7=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =-1\\cdot (-8)\\cdot (-6)=-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544b7ae8-40b2-5ec8-83d6-d19730e6b5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403705} {:answer/id #uuid "2316f0bc-0936-5acf-bd1b-fe6117ca3155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403706} {:answer/id #uuid "1bacfa54-8104-51a9-b2bd-c3c2a3c703ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403707} {:answer/id #uuid "019cfb67-fa2a-5255-b2d3-9c68213d29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403708} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdd38f7-11c0-5b67-b2ce-d02618e9c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "019cfb67-fa2a-5255-b2d3-9c68213d29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403708} :answer-field/id #uuid "199aaf87-46d2-5438-b5f3-ee557ec29887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "166cdda3-01a4-5828-9800-429450a46515" :question/math-academy-id "q-73832" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e79a3a7c2fce5a3e3ec7d838ebe1327dcc5452168f499fad05eea85a08e8f8b0.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(2,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(3+1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $0$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18731f91-5a2b-5b17-a387-eb4cc9a4d188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339346} {:answer/id #uuid "20d59ae8-b207-56f8-a181-059ef32b89f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339410} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e77b72-b0aa-5aa2-80c3-74d69e6d1bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-\\sqrt{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340763} {:answer/id #uuid "e98d2c48-e98a-5bd8-bf7e-745db4c2794a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341189} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c3edd0-3761-5700-a2bb-55bbe3d60703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d59ae8-b207-56f8-a181-059ef32b89f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no intersection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339410} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b9401a-2720-53ab-b421-d5879f6deca9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6253cbd5-f5b5-5898-ac4f-5142a7aaca73" :question/math-academy-id "q-73847" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}=3(x-2)$ with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =3(0-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the equation ${y}^{2}=-6$ has no solutions, we conclude that there are no intersection points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59078a96-a4c1-5355-9bdf-3b0f39c93291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339938} {:answer/id #uuid "836328cc-8806-5c45-bf00-1c3923682d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340310} {:answer/id #uuid "9909d2b8-e7fa-56b5-9269-e5951dc53bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340509} {:answer/id #uuid "a86ba75a-1b29-5797-9053-bf2a3ad7aa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340658} {:answer/id #uuid "b0de169a-6a53-5b29-85e4-09cc91632445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59078a96-a4c1-5355-9bdf-3b0f39c93291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339938} :answer-field/id #uuid "cab68815-1850-5693-b986-be3ac48add05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79f2f5ac-44c5-5b82-9c80-2524e0bd699c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74202" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-4)$ and the line $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/d4/d46a6ed76af5c226e327ba197fe019df0d9085e095ca4781898aba54268a1706.png)\n\nSince the line $x=4$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-1,-4)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $4.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-4|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b6fecf3-af6d-570c-a4e0-5bd2f8943b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339805} {:answer/id #uuid "86531e02-9d87-59a8-99d4-328fa2c86fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340333} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e737ba-76b9-5760-80fc-271f650d20e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341023} {:answer/id #uuid "e47935d1-86c8-5a85-a24b-c428d0150116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341149} {:answer/id #uuid "e58979f5-86fa-57ab-b8b7-766696bc055b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86531e02-9d87-59a8-99d4-328fa2c86fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340333} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8851fb-c592-5f14-9bf4-a48b2c458a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0e530ca-0510-53a9-a606-7cbe24765900" :question/math-academy-id "q-74417" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/eb/eb1176b4a5e2565fceb03e9af652a6ae875f5ee678c103405045fcebc36342e1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x+2$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+3 & =1(x+1) \\\\ y+3 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+2 \\\\ y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2 & =x-2 \\\\ -x-x & =-2-2 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,-3)$ and $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2$ is $3\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f7ce0c-16e5-531a-94b9-547622df3f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339767} {:answer/id #uuid "569cbb0a-f99c-5355-8f53-bdc678082993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339923} {:answer/id #uuid "8688ee8a-8219-5c84-a042-632559c1a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340337} {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} {:answer/id #uuid "ae765e2d-062d-5b17-8c52-1eef7d4ff32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} :answer-field/id #uuid "73585de0-1b70-548d-9d76-12f3a61d673b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7220b7e3-9cce-5102-874c-014511b9e7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74439" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85555bce-debf-5165-ba6a-3c232deeeb0a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264dc817-12f6-57bb-98c7-13484b763445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339460} {:answer/id #uuid "5237867d-730f-5989-9d77-b64a864aae4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339884} {:answer/id #uuid "a070bbce-1d86-53d3-854d-47717ba43e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340580} {:answer/id #uuid "b535ca1d-ad11-5b54-be0c-efb5196653c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340771} {:answer/id #uuid "d00ae2d9-df1a-5189-bde1-3e4432444aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "264dc817-12f6-57bb-98c7-13484b763445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339460} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d4cc48-92f4-5197-9d4d-51927a8d2422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea168c27-f5a7-59a8-ab75-3dcd4e8a5c95" :question/math-academy-id "q-74575" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, and then factoring it as a difference of squares, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1} & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1)(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1)}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31d92381-0c28-53fc-b918-99c16544c201" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a4b5fa4-5e85-51e5-9b82-2d9649982cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342994} {:answer/id #uuid "b211db82-e6c0-5369-afda-a7d6f0604094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343801} {:answer/id #uuid "c09f0e7e-ade8-567e-9bdb-c40ef89b86f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343929} {:answer/id #uuid "c2604448-70cd-5a27-872f-a0bae07bc1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343947} {:answer/id #uuid "c5724e21-1987-5dc1-86a5-a1cfebf33b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c09f0e7e-ade8-567e-9bdb-c40ef89b86f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343929} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c5d8f1-2e5c-5cee-821c-064fe215366f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7d8403d-1335-583d-b92f-e71dbc01f5b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-7463" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5a933346a903b6712b3df53fa2f241b070ef80146078b96b8f4360ae6650233.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and has radius $r=3.$ So, the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "678ba6cb-b259-5a25-858c-9db161a34ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Real Number by a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f03ab48-2456-5848-b330-165fc1457620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{13}-\\frac{27}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342633} {:answer/id #uuid "308ea711-b018-58dc-97ca-c9410d45e894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{13}+\\frac{18}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342642} {:answer/id #uuid "40c99ea8-5f63-5b3f-8d23-6bbb3883c1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{13}-\\frac{18}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342772} {:answer/id #uuid "5285e1d7-9b84-5240-a612-f16959ab2ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{13}+\\frac{18}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342923} {:answer/id #uuid "efeacb6c-de66-5a0b-8e29-6f0916164222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{13}-\\frac{18}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efeacb6c-de66-5a0b-8e29-6f0916164222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{13}-\\frac{18}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344346} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9045964-73ba-507e-9088-a8e8656fd086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35212040-01f3-5be8-a8f4-ec8be3d798dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-74648" :question/problem "$\\frac{9}{-3+2\\text{i}}=$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator is $-3+2\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-3-2\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-3-2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{-3+2\\text{i}} & =\\frac{9}{-3+2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-3-2\\text{i}}{-3-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{9+6\\text{i}-6\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{9-4(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{9+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{13}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{13}=-\\frac{27}{13}-\\frac{18}{13}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2902b205-4508-55c6-9a68-f57d3afc9eab" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values of Trigonometric Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d46c16-ac4e-57b4-ae20-20086616c19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342277} {:answer/id #uuid "24e78c28-e4f9-5764-a34b-418c159f48ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342548} {:answer/id #uuid "461de0e9-b9bb-5f6f-af7c-df1d3be102dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342814} {:answer/id #uuid "80b774e9-0533-56ca-9013-aade87186d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343348} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2368da-9551-54cc-b612-4a5cc5d70024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b774e9-0533-56ca-9013-aade87186d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343348} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4a9942c-2ff0-547a-87bb-c11cc79e965c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b81c92b6-aaf0-5164-b094-1fd4bab4c1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-7473" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c348231-faac-5f15-ba88-4a5fcff9895b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393433} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbe61fb-3b9a-5b63-aefd-518ef08c77c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393434} {:answer/id #uuid "741f5285-7e52-5afa-8fd8-e7ffe7bb3ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393435} {:answer/id #uuid "3e486dbe-f3f0-57e0-8019-075933a8c6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393436} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1aa637-1399-5be1-8754-cfd06e8753a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e1aa637-1399-5be1-8754-cfd06e8753a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393437} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d0c3af7-ad97-5864-bf2c-f087f946215b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96c9c6b4-4b9d-5bd8-af24-21ff4fd8965e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74762" :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]) & =T(4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=4T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e37951b-03d5-5d76-8207-ab35ada60274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342489} {:answer/id #uuid "6884287b-be82-539e-a37e-d742e488e1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343130} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd306d4-ea1c-535c-800d-cf76b50ffc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343199} {:answer/id #uuid "bd29d454-d450-573b-992f-b35c8afcb1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343901} {:answer/id #uuid "e53aba8b-8d6b-5204-b078-e2ccc56fd5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e37951b-03d5-5d76-8207-ab35ada60274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342489} :answer-field/id #uuid "761eb299-8f17-5fa6-8798-80c42abd8f3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7ac2355-a1ae-54c5-9e5b-62d94023dff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-74775" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+7{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{1}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+hw)=T(kv)+T(hw)=kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+7{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+7T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =3T({v}_{1})+7[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9149cf2-edbf-544d-9c0f-90c2f55edc73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}63 \\\\ 51 \\\\ -42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398991} {:answer/id #uuid "1f624e49-b4d8-5481-a766-3d48a04ffb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-42 \\\\ -34 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398992} {:answer/id #uuid "f08777ee-7bc2-552d-b942-d0cc1bce2816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-21 \\\\ -17 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398993} {:answer/id #uuid "05991325-fa66-564d-8673-e9450a190897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-63 \\\\ -51 \\\\ 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398994} {:answer/id #uuid "91bbea6d-c7c9-55fe-bfcc-d43e664cab17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91bbea6d-c7c9-55fe-bfcc-d43e664cab17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398995} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bb35426-6c4d-5808-82e6-12f94e6cdf85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e4e42a9-e81d-5610-8794-d5c4cdbe24ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-74781" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2} & =37{v}_{1}+37{v}_{2}+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37(0)+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=T(2{v}_{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{2}) \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e520dc67-3b63-5ca7-959b-f642d85529d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213c55cc-b400-5377-9058-bd34f39b7911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342513} {:answer/id #uuid "5edce411-6450-5a72-885e-5f5697bcd90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343038} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f95458-4bb8-5ad3-a0dd-d8a9d0a8a332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344125} {:answer/id #uuid "f393e9e6-4bbf-5736-a0fd-bfd2be688d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344382} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c06a87-a77a-5a9d-83dc-507977b8cfcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f95458-4bb8-5ad3-a0dd-d8a9d0a8a332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344125} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd0f00d-f655-5fb4-8bb8-b431451aae9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69666851-9696-5bfc-814b-85edf6949681" :question/math-academy-id "q-74997" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,2t⟩,$ $|r(3)|=$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function at $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨3(3)-1,\\,2(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(3)| & =\\sqrt{{(8)}^{2}+{(6)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+36} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31d92381-0c28-53fc-b918-99c16544c201" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de0b51d-faae-5f32-8b30-e73e2b2c0fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-8)^{2}+(y-8)^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342487} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8707c2-b9cf-533d-8890-ecd70d5f9668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-8)^{2}+(y-8)^{2}=64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342500} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3a7d8a-a190-55b5-abba-57feb7f63706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343000} {:answer/id #uuid "aa192b6b-de25-5786-bfa0-a411d58974ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343739} {:answer/id #uuid "ce63d0d2-a9ed-56f1-944e-db0ac83ce962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa192b6b-de25-5786-bfa0-a411d58974ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343739} :answer-field/id #uuid "778dc285-85ea-521b-b311-2dacc1fc9f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "766c92af-918a-5c5a-817f-df1487f3ad21" :question/math-academy-id "q-7513" :question/problem "![](images/78/78894ff88f2697711873cbf191af60a4f2ae53d31d5ad0337c6cfba79b636b2c.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and has radius $r=8.$ So, the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={8}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Power and Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e4799b-7cd0-5cb1-9266-f051a077fd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342510} {:answer/id #uuid "80b2d167-5da5-5f35-9fe6-52013ee0e068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343347} {:answer/id #uuid "851e346c-b574-577c-81ee-b3cad8815d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343379} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8e36d8-288a-5694-bc61-5e66300fe6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344049} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c0f3fe-29c2-5c13-bb47-1de7b39c108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8e36d8-288a-5694-bc61-5e66300fe6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344049} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc8cd83e-9262-564b-8790-28e8d46fbcb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c99b596-c493-5824-b816-7b21709913d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-75723" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1$ for $n>1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Power and Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d96f2a-a6cf-514a-9602-99435b8bdfe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342457} {:answer/id #uuid "4d63d5c1-38d4-522b-8bbf-b80798d00ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342874} {:answer/id #uuid "963803c8-3216-5e4b-8238-3bdffa538bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343552} {:answer/id #uuid "ae342e10-fe6c-5811-a324-24bc29260cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343768} {:answer/id #uuid "ca26cee5-cce5-58e7-b675-c41eb34e2fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d63d5c1-38d4-522b-8bbf-b80798d00ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342874} :answer-field/id #uuid "392e876d-cf8e-55a2-8af7-e0b2afaa011c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0526be40-3312-57da-9d43-a0847eb52729" :question/math-academy-id "q-75727" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703429e4-f6ee-5ae0-b54b-621a3b3aa87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343202} {:answer/id #uuid "a374971b-c760-5d83-adbf-3b1936e47374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343673} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd32382-778d-546c-b434-bc2f6f15182c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343889} {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} {:answer/id #uuid "f93e23d5-adfb-539c-932c-d93d3f7f5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd80722c-1778-5d31-a005-82a0a1a70cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343905} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e62a385-bb95-5754-89d7-c5b01db0db60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "263b5643-f9a7-58b5-b4f2-be4d8aef81b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75862" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 & |\\times 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ 2x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $y$ term that can cancel with the $-y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 &  \\\\ 2x-y=4 & |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ -2x+y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{y} & =6 \\\\ -2x+\\cancel{y} & =-4 \\\\ (6x-2x) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =4 \\\\ 2(\\frac{1}{2})-y & =4 \\\\ 1-y & =4 \\\\ y & =1-4 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "403fead1-28dc-51e8-883e-1ea78fb58c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342768} {:answer/id #uuid "51524fcb-5371-5134-98fd-7bc0b95c2398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342910} {:answer/id #uuid "6d345723-89f4-5351-84cf-b5bdbe44c4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343174} {:answer/id #uuid "bcca82d0-a051-5fde-94e1-fbf46764a754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343898} {:answer/id #uuid "dce93094-4d77-5ba6-b201-1d213789909e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d345723-89f4-5351-84cf-b5bdbe44c4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343174} :answer-field/id #uuid "db497c06-807c-5ce4-a68f-3dd5b362ea6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19f3a0d4-a9e3-5752-9f3d-c41b8d14446d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75924" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x} & =4 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}) & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{x}-x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{x} & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{\\cancel{x}}-\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{x}} & =4x \\\\ x+7-(1-x) & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7-(1-x) & =4x \\\\ 2x+6 & =4x \\\\ 4x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=3\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2685fdf5-b2ef-5419-a715-1c74ce9389ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342565} {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} {:answer/id #uuid "75251e7a-9236-5e4c-9b53-7cfd86320732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343251} {:answer/id #uuid "978d082a-57ab-588e-8901-810af078103c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343568} {:answer/id #uuid "9f71a6a2-9c4d-582e-afe9-1afe12b68ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c73ff8-7bb1-58f5-b9d4-61756b11ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342568} :answer-field/id #uuid "4171e3f8-5f6b-53b2-9b4c-3f49129ae323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8936d7-9acb-5f42-aac4-919b57567005" :question/math-academy-id "q-75925" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{5x}=\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $5x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{5x} & =\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5 \\\\ 5x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{5x}) & =5x\\cdot (\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5) \\\\ 5x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{5x} & =5x\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5x\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{5x}} & =\\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{\\cancel{5x}}+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3-20x+25x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-20x+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3+5x \\\\ 5x-x & =-2-3 \\\\ 4x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{5}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a7452c-e3f9-526d-82be-805b605b288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342420} {:answer/id #uuid "2377a0c3-c1bd-53c7-93bf-77cbc38fa76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342532} {:answer/id #uuid "7c304e6a-4939-558c-9504-44c7f57d0bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343307} {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} {:answer/id #uuid "cef19a74-15fe-5db4-b7f1-7bf698a07ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b825111c-c17e-5e28-b1d7-789b226b6fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9331d1-6e4e-5569-b803-cfc624eda5cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64167e5a-a3e2-55d1-956c-8945cc13aa71" :question/math-academy-id "q-75941" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}=\\frac{m+4}{15m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,5m,$ and $15m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,5m,15m)=15m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $15m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m} & =\\frac{m+4}{15m} \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+3\\cdot 5m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{\\cancel{3m}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{5m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{\\cancel{5m}}) & =\\cancel{15m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{\\cancel{15m}}) \\\\ 5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4 \\\\ 10m-15+3m+15 & =m+4 \\\\ 13m & =m+4 \\\\ 12m & =4 \\\\ m & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2368068-c473-5919-aff6-53a14b246b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396122} {:answer/id #uuid "801b88de-1112-5c33-bd1b-ddb864de2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396123} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd49079-798b-58f7-8490-cd6fb613a033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396124} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b72ba2-9df1-5acc-b385-173c4e36f9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396125} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef4ba21-7e9c-5949-8c09-0a45d37f8298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "480a616c-8c80-5f81-b3d5-aa7488c0665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7596" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=18.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=18,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caded66-c544-50e4-92e7-ee68a0ea1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342480} {:answer/id #uuid "4a78637a-f1b3-54af-bede-ea45a41273cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342850} {:answer/id #uuid "85bacb3b-5422-572c-8abd-1f411ccc1aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343384} {:answer/id #uuid "985ece06-b158-505f-bbf1-5db5ef384f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343575} {:answer/id #uuid "efb241ee-41d8-5ae3-b24b-55570171313d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1caded66-c544-50e4-92e7-ee68a0ea1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342480} :answer-field/id #uuid "3233d4f6-e0f4-5864-aa06-929e37d35a50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7877136-a6fe-51f9-a9fa-720076528d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-75962" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7db323bd-027d-5a72-8744-915dc9466d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399052} {:answer/id #uuid "363b9e46-0e55-5119-8918-a99ef9c17cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399053} {:answer/id #uuid "c64155b0-249e-5565-ad41-5aa4d4de25a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399054} {:answer/id #uuid "ab114369-abaf-5996-96e1-bc98ccf909b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399055} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd9f381-647a-53f8-8916-ebcaa02fb734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c64155b0-249e-5565-ad41-5aa4d4de25a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399054} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad20513b-7f6b-51c1-b3e8-54f31e6cfac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfd92570-bd08-5565-9c64-935de2ba2369" :question/math-academy-id "q-76110" :question/problem "![](images/76/760ddc657e8372a3860e2553e4f2b713a8b12544586c708e5f2c1922462567a8.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=3$  but this is already excluded.  \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals. \n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-3,4)$ and $(0,5)$ contain the excluded point $x=3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  $(-\\infty ,0)$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-3$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee3d757-9c11-529a-853f-800224ed53f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{9}+(y-2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342631} {:answer/id #uuid "4af06850-a9e7-5958-87c8-eca061d4a95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342855} {:answer/id #uuid "7f56ea67-57ce-5a9c-afd1-b8cf934f86a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343332} {:answer/id #uuid "a8995e69-24a2-5f25-9f1f-f6152bb8456c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343723} {:answer/id #uuid "d265a133-79db-517a-af76-0eeaff4e4bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f56ea67-57ce-5a9c-afd1-b8cf934f86a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f297f988-c765-53b7-92f6-ff0a624bb3de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25c67443-abb8-58a6-bbc2-b7f961d0e0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-7619" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2bc4b82c6cfae63026f221dd49cdc74a9ce3884443e933cae02c36f6d732707e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,4),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=1,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e3f3b4-8dfa-515c-90b1-09fb19d68144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square with Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707a4cfe-d34e-5500-a601-9753b554d97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(1,-\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{5}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403838} {:answer/id #uuid "0043bbe1-8c13-5357-841d-e795a04df199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-1,-\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403839} {:answer/id #uuid "13f05de5-d7d8-5d9c-9e5d-ac75c76fa048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(1,\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403840} {:answer/id #uuid "1705b14f-f5fa-5468-b93d-33bcdcdbb387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ radius: $\\frac{7}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403841} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1a32ca-3acb-5d7d-b8ad-8a49ebecf6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-1,\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{5}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13f05de5-d7d8-5d9c-9e5d-ac75c76fa048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(1,\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403840} :answer-field/id #uuid "64251e03-03e9-594a-89a0-0a7ae7fb1367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64257e64-8ece-56eb-a9da-46dae15085ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-76374" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}-4x+2{y}^{2}-y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x+2{y}^{2}-y & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+({y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}+{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{16} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(1,\\frac{1}{4})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4e2843-1069-5d5a-bc66-06cba791d839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403845} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8990c4-efb5-5c40-8ee3-12b22397b390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403846} {:answer/id #uuid "05b80062-5158-567e-9a1e-8cfd58f9c9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403847} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aba08c-b98e-5626-b761-ad0e976d4fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403848} {:answer/id #uuid "4d59c083-d711-58a1-a0a9-72a889180cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e2843-1069-5d5a-bc66-06cba791d839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403845} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df721af-644e-5936-b5f6-55c036b03023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9bca935-b9bf-540f-a535-f5de3e3b987f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ 4+6(y+2)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =32 \\\\ 3(y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-2-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ and $(0,-2+\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf73c45-3794-517e-8928-e6a008fbf5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396150} {:answer/id #uuid "65c8299f-0cca-5cc7-9f93-89e41aaadb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396151} {:answer/id #uuid "a0731f5e-f8ac-5439-971f-5fe3dacb20b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396152} {:answer/id #uuid "75a247b7-4469-5470-aaaf-1d9daf6fb7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396153} {:answer/id #uuid "6bac02c1-368e-5372-9c25-9ed8a24fc65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a247b7-4469-5470-aaaf-1d9daf6fb7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396153} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e54ba7-369d-573d-a4bf-97c68ce7831a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86de3c58-7ec8-5280-8df9-9b9e42af2697" :question/math-academy-id "q-7644" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 3+(y+3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-3\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-6\\,,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-6)$ and $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152117 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d89e619-199e-55ac-8eca-9d77fffa7706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343023} {:answer/id #uuid "a00fafbc-22ed-52d6-8f01-31dffa057768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343651} {:answer/id #uuid "da747809-5242-5b07-9971-7285cceb6b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150(y+1)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344157} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3e407c-9b56-57b2-833f-24e819c942f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344298} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9d1027-57d3-5a23-9b93-f847e9338320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150(y+1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d89e619-199e-55ac-8eca-9d77fffa7706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343023} :answer-field/id #uuid "81b1b5e2-12f3-59b8-8a06-c39c41de6a36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fe6c820-9683-5a7a-b33c-5f323f0359af" :question/math-academy-id "q-76457" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $10(y+1)^{6}$ and $15(y+1)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(10,15):$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,…$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(10,15)=30.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y+1)^{6}$ and $(y+1)^{4},$ we raise $(y+1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y+1)^{6},\\,(y+1)^{4})=(y+1)^{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10(y+1)^{6},\\,15(y+1)^{4})=30\\cdot (y+1)^{6}=30(y+1)^{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8ffd5a2-cc34-54ff-9dff-23dc300f6355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403859} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c7d047-707f-5931-9d5e-9a61b60ed7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403860} {:answer/id #uuid "f83190fc-dc05-547f-bf1b-da6929340b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403861} {:answer/id #uuid "9180fb06-deed-5622-9444-9a79d51d72e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm 2\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403862} {:answer/id #uuid "3643a336-82e3-5461-b332-ac90d59fc7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83190fc-dc05-547f-bf1b-da6929340b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403861} :answer-field/id #uuid "651c5733-a40e-59c7-b992-154c6de03a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4ad8b92-e3b5-5a56-bfac-e69e0f571229" :question/math-academy-id "q-7646" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y=10$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y & =10 \\\\ (0)^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4(0)+4y & =10 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y & =10 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =5+1 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1-\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-1+\\sqrt{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152119 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57496436-8bd0-567e-8c37-ed584849ce1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0605e45f-3e5a-5d33-a682-7100aa2a8ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120{x}^{4}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342294} {:answer/id #uuid "11f59668-bc57-5e29-9418-45b2197c07c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342398} {:answer/id #uuid "b54ac959-62ea-5199-9f5f-2f2c86259b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{17}(y+2)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343825} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0e194f-07bb-5959-8ccc-8a01a267e3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{4}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344013} {:answer/id #uuid "d99fcded-2ea6-5c5d-aeda-dffd3b0f7c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d99fcded-2ea6-5c5d-aeda-dffd3b0f7c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344149} :answer-field/id #uuid "27415d0b-998e-58eb-abc2-faf32c0e2a98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "288f9264-3705-5ed8-96fa-98d1f57e896b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76475" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $20{x}^{7}(y+2),$ $\\,10{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},$ and $12{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(20,10,12):$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,\\,100,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,\\,50,\\,60,\\,70,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(20,10,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of ${x}^{7}(y+2),{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},$ and ${x}^{4}(y+2)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $x$ and $(y+2)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For ${x}^{7},\\,{x}^{6},$ and ${x}^{4},$ the largest power is $7.$\n\n- For $(y+2),(y+2)^{3},$ and $(y+2)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\nNow, we raise $x$ and $(y+2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({x}^{7}(y+2),\\,{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},\\,{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2})={x}^{7}\\cdot (y+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(20{x}^{7}(y+2),\\,10{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},\\,12{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot {x}^{7}\\cdot (y+2)^{3} & = \\\\ 60{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11080520-591d-552f-a2b5-376652328032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342388} {:answer/id #uuid "20d23199-1707-5c8a-9c95-20a93346eafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342508} {:answer/id #uuid "23574e97-dff3-5da7-b5f5-3cada9731425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342530} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1702de-fd43-50d8-a686-676b7d54dfb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343884} {:answer/id #uuid "f9127ae7-e1de-5695-9e51-049f89115cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9127ae7-e1de-5695-9e51-049f89115cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344429} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b740c15-4310-51b1-b74c-cbe3e504200f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c61332e0-ca58-508b-96a8-0bfc3b0688b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76773" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca9c6640b6cb239ae19b195731d8f605adae8826f66e10b075f7b0ad4a60690f.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+4} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+4} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(x+4) & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\,x+4\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x=\\sqrt{3}(2)=2\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =x+4=2+4=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a0075a5-c212-5332-8254-ebacab091f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342847} {:answer/id #uuid "579bc87b-bc15-55d2-9b88-65df7df9f603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{22}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{22}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342969} {:answer/id #uuid "83360fb2-68cd-5170-8292-0cf16f4cf726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343361} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6b13d2-42b5-583d-8200-ed5b37808ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344182} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1a1f8b-f9f2-5fba-9b53-4159efa314f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a0075a5-c212-5332-8254-ebacab091f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342847} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6797f23-b08c-5f40-9334-1a28a80d78c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec9090ba-bb1f-5578-9f23-889023ae580e" :question/math-academy-id "q-7692" :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡{22}^{∘}-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡{22}^{∘}-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\cdot {22}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{44}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab5b44a-6f14-5d8c-b994-0ff723092bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234586} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b4c72-e340-52b5-be53-6903f63361a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234611} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe36434-9519-583f-9b69-c587e4937715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234866} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1ea7c1-61ca-56ba-a7d9-91573e3d6a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234869} {:answer/id #uuid "f46ca5b6-1fd3-597e-9657-e4197a5f9d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab5b44a-6f14-5d8c-b994-0ff723092bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234586} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa48e98-a238-5102-8717-85e261cb4e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adcdd06d-1586-51df-9df4-c005428c239d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77451" :question/problem "![](images/28/28faea3a553c505a8a5ab9bc7dd6e784dd23a47c336c70d31218fbe249d57cff.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/52/52421034c2919620a0a9aae844db0eba2d396e5d6f1152d69bee40ff56c4d8ad.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=-3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7e817a0-edcf-54ca-ba4c-ffe91af04675" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Height of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5975ee7c-248f-52aa-92e6-7307c035348b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/389163fbb07fef4fc36fca5dce7cbe86cd4830d810b0210fe47c2b7cedd4b8c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393739} {:answer/id #uuid "69276a71-4933-599e-b951-26b490c52cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e2b6cde516dc1737124576ffdc212331e3ad74f728f47aece5911ed04cf7f5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393740} {:answer/id #uuid "df0291cf-6864-5a2c-a3de-e53242dc7ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/7720d00d73ea3d4b4cf4905cf57ec12a3953caeb135272ec2d5283dad1479efe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393741} {:answer/id #uuid "5358c8ed-0c53-52b2-9dde-eca396e3f641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c1ecb1d567916c708cc6bbbd394517835e86b4105ae045cf5ef9490b181900fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393742} {:answer/id #uuid "3896dd03-da28-567c-b00f-2916b9f8afe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5f4e50dfc88fdb9109961e2cef0dc0b1f7968d7b588e2f944450ac2969075bd7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5975ee7c-248f-52aa-92e6-7307c035348b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/389163fbb07fef4fc36fca5dce7cbe86cd4830d810b0210fe47c2b7cedd4b8c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393739} :answer-field/id #uuid "8715746d-70c4-5d7d-a61a-15d532009b48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "813db841-ac50-5fa6-a6b6-270cab223353" :question/math-academy-id "q-77606" :question/problem "![](images/35/3507450dd4d81fb14269ec651cc3f93ef52d615797ac3e6f4f779a0df98debe2.png)\n\nGiven the triangle $△ABC$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the altitude  corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}?$" :question/worked-solution "The height (or altitude)  corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $C$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{AB},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{AB}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below:\n\n![](images/38/389163fbb07fef4fc36fca5dce7cbe86cd4830d810b0210fe47c2b7cedd4b8c8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078618 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f4dadb-28d5-582a-b2ed-8ed6faec0d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345421} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8dfa95-d908-5058-8424-56e86a5e56fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345459} {:answer/id #uuid "1064f00d-bc7f-5c7a-b35a-043241be2aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345491} {:answer/id #uuid "89f10bff-844a-5272-bf2d-066f640d14c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346550} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3cacc9-07ac-5e10-a702-e49578e0954d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd3cacc9-07ac-5e10-a702-e49578e0954d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347292} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ea8fd09-6e18-5c7d-9352-b57872f05fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c3a8a5e-9f27-59d8-ab5a-abb566b24c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-78030" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34fd903a-fa34-57b0-a098-08fa1cbd4f95" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084d51f3-27f2-5017-9b38-8362f1786e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\times 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345418} {:answer/id #uuid "1f19011d-f9d7-51a2-b47b-062470e495ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\times 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345624} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5ee179-ea1e-5b7b-8108-6d7ffb3f3335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\times 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346429} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7b8f9f-5bd0-5fbb-946c-210bcc263174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\times 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346469} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc997d2-fa6c-5774-9ee3-84c1a35725c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\times 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f7b8f9f-5bd0-5fbb-946c-210bcc263174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\times 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346469} :answer-field/id #uuid "48ba6808-50d1-57d5-9c23-1cf6910fb334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d16d6d10-2374-5a1c-9c0b-fb79f9319483" :question/math-academy-id "q-7809" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $F$, where $F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $F$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns.  Hence,  its dimensions are  $1\\times 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aeaace-3dee-5861-928d-6a23090d00f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345366} {:answer/id #uuid "01c007c5-b958-5f05-bcdb-d16eb849a4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345367} {:answer/id #uuid "02100b32-fbcc-582d-903e-bf23af1e8c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345368} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6767c-d2f1-526e-934e-fbbf34e09777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347068} {:answer/id #uuid "f40cbba4-ff28-567c-bde7-f1a1ff700f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f40cbba4-ff28-567c-bde7-f1a1ff700f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347512} :answer-field/id #uuid "01de2c48-cbc5-5a34-8784-842c895031e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2c6cc4-6d0f-5ca4-bd69-989208f699b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78132" :question/problem "![](images/89/890e7eaf519a65ca19d4a90b604e7d7a8ab7054635296c4b08f000546f23b545.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $-2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of  $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and III only\".\n\n![](images/7c/7c96c401ddae0049859fd5608cb08c6ce9ae8a2e6c3db69d7d4b379b3824094e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083206 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d6c920-989f-5ce5-a8d5-c2abc399051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345915} {:answer/id #uuid "5b65b5d5-07d7-5975-9ee4-291d6b399353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346130} {:answer/id #uuid "9e973f9e-81b5-5854-88b0-98066c9a699d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346735} {:answer/id #uuid "b2220b9a-c972-5a58-a399-44ce9b253549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346905} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfc5434-346a-59f7-b3db-0e599e3994ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d6c920-989f-5ce5-a8d5-c2abc399051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345915} :answer-field/id #uuid "13539e3e-82b2-55bb-b37b-e5e32ff876f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8280a162-8b28-5252-92be-7c19e3ebbf0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78379" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+12x-12y=12?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+12x-12y & =12 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-12y-3{x}^{2}+12x & =12 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-6y]-3[{x}^{2}-4x] & =12 \\\\ 2[(y-3)^{2}-9]-3[(x-2)^{2}-4] & =12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-18-3(x-2)^{2}+12 & =12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-3(x-2)^{2} & =12+18-12 \\\\ 2(y-3)^{2}-3(x-2)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(2,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083299 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0703d799-0111-55e4-99f9-70c3eb59a8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345414} {:answer/id #uuid "40758a02-7b8c-5d72-ab58-a8d20d28afdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345905} {:answer/id #uuid "63992de9-ec6d-51ab-a206-cde915c80422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346218} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb31ca7-c07d-505f-bbbb-56822f2e4ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346736} {:answer/id #uuid "f59b0784-1475-5ee1-bbd4-044ae1258144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0703d799-0111-55e4-99f9-70c3eb59a8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345414} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f2ac7a2-8856-589e-8887-844636305c61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "209d3fb4-3f5a-5f61-958c-85386d790787" :question/math-academy-id "q-78871" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-20x-2y=-47?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-20x-2y & =-47 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x-{y}^{2}-2y & =-47 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]-[{y}^{2}+2y] & =-47 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]-[(y+1)^{2}-1] & =-47 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50-(y+1)^{2}+1 & =-47 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =-47+50-1 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(5,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ad9106-6449-5425-9ea8-8c4d982508a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345373} {:answer/id #uuid "45dc42bc-9e4d-55b0-9f61-b8cbcec2623e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345960} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9b883-5db0-5670-b24b-baadb64f5beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4fe63e-62d3-5e7b-87ef-17e62dfe7c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346988} {:answer/id #uuid "e4746b2e-d4c3-51e0-b47c-2a5f28dfd1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9b883-5db0-5670-b24b-baadb64f5beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbb2f12-0ff8-5ad2-898f-75f1bc5be3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "858cfde7-5900-59e1-9b39-5d78acc22281" :question/math-academy-id "q-78950" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-{sec}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbf0b70-eb73-5737-870e-2b2a733a604d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345593} {:answer/id #uuid "22ea9738-c711-5104-a068-657f18af36ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345664} {:answer/id #uuid "58320d87-d5f1-5831-b7ba-1fc8e51312dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346104} {:answer/id #uuid "7d80556e-d68c-5841-8c9b-4fac2591689a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346449} {:answer/id #uuid "de267612-3488-5cfc-bf84-8951e79d0127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d80556e-d68c-5841-8c9b-4fac2591689a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346449} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb795487-a850-5ed5-84bc-cdf04a7dfdc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2921b965-ad16-58ab-8156-5829abb484a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78954" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2,\\,y={t}^{7}+2.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=7{t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7{t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2)={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7{t}^{2})}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14t}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=2}=\\frac{14}{(2)^{3}}=\\frac{14}{8}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b374944-d47a-526e-b651-a9bdd179683c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The locus has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405189} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc41c5f-d7b6-5d05-8b47-2e4e472399bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405190} {:answer/id #uuid "d443e6bd-6dee-5d3a-b69a-1e6b48338e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1-\\sqrt{11},0)$ and $(1+\\sqrt{11},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405191} {:answer/id #uuid "73af1a99-304f-5788-9f9f-7c5af517eb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405192} {:answer/id #uuid "eec08ca2-05c0-5cfd-a7e9-7540222dfb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{11},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b374944-d47a-526e-b651-a9bdd179683c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The locus has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405189} :answer-field/id #uuid "a56e38c9-b83e-56e9-bf4c-ec09e761bb76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f43a31ca-f582-5ca4-a303-c0af1e9c11d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-79044" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the real locus ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12=0$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the locus and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2x+6\\cdot 0+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1+12 & =0+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+12 & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-1)^{2}=-11.$ Therefore, the locus has no $x$-intercepts."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad4bfcf-ac83-5c47-928b-5b1a7fc0f104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183674} {:answer/id #uuid "df3e3991-8f50-5de8-b75b-5960efe17e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183675} {:answer/id #uuid "f2572169-df3d-5c99-975a-9f3f678ee3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183676} {:answer/id #uuid "f28c8896-b9fe-5342-9cba-cebeee74006f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183677} {:answer/id #uuid "095c622d-5db2-5a48-942e-b312abb3f7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183678}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d980f41-06e1-5562-995c-dd718d6a8c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183673} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081bfb29-fde1-5481-b123-8eac743f086f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399099} {:answer/id #uuid "51e08aac-9339-503c-9a66-b74268d4d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399100} {:answer/id #uuid "03fedd1c-29b4-5722-b1e7-b3c046e5b66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399101} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ce8598-f6eb-5e70-b2e5-0d95b7a5f429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399102} {:answer/id #uuid "6704d952-af2f-5f8a-a722-c90281d08d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fedd1c-29b4-5722-b1e7-b3c046e5b66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399101} :answer-field/id #uuid "1848e56b-8e02-510d-9f75-0f28e4f3c082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d92d6b12-34ec-5e74-a8fa-e75755bbf55a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79188" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae6a514e0d9cde3db3c3b1dfa94e3804b5469dd5fb99b40a94684e88b573ec38.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units up. Hence, $D=3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+3=6.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f632187-9689-5dcb-8607-35b8892e2f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345770} {:answer/id #uuid "482f9eb4-7bcd-514b-8d01-90bf4b1be5b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345971} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9bd68d-398a-5f81-9d3b-2d620308a60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346702} {:answer/id #uuid "d8491bfd-8d7f-547e-bf7c-f3bb48bb9729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347256} {:answer/id #uuid "f907922f-5150-5e85-afd6-29d01f5304a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "482f9eb4-7bcd-514b-8d01-90bf4b1be5b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345971} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a244fb-0550-5775-8987-a65a296a9de2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06e8a49e-e560-5c26-89ae-564e1f5a8b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79294" :question/problem "![](images/40/407f89e40691ddaefd912c97dc9e8b15a08e0120ad2cf1c9a277417cd8380e98.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-4)$ and passes through the point $(7,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(7,5),$ we substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-4)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 16 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =16-4 \\\\ y & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004712b6-f610-5c1b-85fc-ea7f5dac42b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3a-2}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345356} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9c9fb7-186e-5b20-a5ae-9968ec5bb0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(a+1)}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345629} {:answer/id #uuid "5f647f49-d7f6-51ed-a318-7ae05732cfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(a+2)}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346174} {:answer/id #uuid "6edfa3c8-26af-59e1-83da-3da4f171bbe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3a+2}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346319} {:answer/id #uuid "8714236f-9c2b-58c3-a954-b1f5b9039774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-3a}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6edfa3c8-26af-59e1-83da-3da4f171bbe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3a+2}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346319} :answer-field/id #uuid "c86f26bc-cd60-5415-a7c8-50d3d722dac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "381e5b72-d586-538b-b748-3282ad434825" :question/math-academy-id "q-79545" :question/problem "Write $3+\\frac{2}{a}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{a}{a}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a}{a}+\\frac{2}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a}{a}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a+2}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17060fa7-3524-5310-9dfd-dc07b4e8832c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345555} {:answer/id #uuid "8e08b27c-99f7-56ac-8b9f-96e0b8ceef2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+2k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346586} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae7946-dd0b-5491-a675-76007cd01c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+k}{k+3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346842} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dcd984-a6b9-52d2-bddf-c9136d14c8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t-2k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347043} {:answer/id #uuid "ce330a49-fcee-5fd6-95ef-e1da5c0f2f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+2k}{k+3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e08b27c-99f7-56ac-8b9f-96e0b8ceef2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t+2k}{2(k+3t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346586} :answer-field/id #uuid "7650f596-e2e4-5bc7-be67-53414e53752a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e830cbad-9670-547b-94f1-5280434ab395" :question/math-academy-id "q-79551" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{k}{k+3t}.$" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2(k+3t)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{k}{k+3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{k}{k+3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t}{2(k+3t)}+\\frac{2k}{2(k+3t)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t+2k}{2(k+3t)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218cb034-3e85-59b7-a903-56bfc598b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given options is correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345650} {:answer/id #uuid "5f26a8c0-2af0-5bee-a1cd-0bf2f5569905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346172} {:answer/id #uuid "6db0d065-e8ef-5454-9cf2-b8d92a429fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346309} {:answer/id #uuid "afcb4450-4ae6-54b7-b7e5-2a45c0d057a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346883} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fffe14-90a9-51ed-b231-0b2a4af2d1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8fffe14-90a9-51ed-b231-0b2a4af2d1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347102} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a7510d2-2b7e-5e14-bd9b-6604c31ce57e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de44d5ab-abd2-5362-b1b7-cef838348808" :question/math-academy-id "q-80107" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1f31073c260d8111cca3d5de1ec6db3e0f7f76a13c9a41a71b5512acd17f704.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above can be the graph of the polynomial $y=-{x}^{5}+4{x}^{2}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=-{x}^{5}+4{x}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nThe degree $(n=5)$ is odd and the leading coefficient $({a}_{5}=-1)$ is negative. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOnly the graph $D$ satisfies these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77dc6fdd-2db7-55bf-abb5-b59442a1464c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346402} {:answer/id #uuid "b477c45c-b737-549b-91b5-d1091460ac32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346923} {:answer/id #uuid "d114ac37-9962-5408-9d6e-05d942670183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347182} {:answer/id #uuid "e341ede8-046b-52c2-b8b6-aa4cf9fe8ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347350} {:answer/id #uuid "fe903f8c-2fae-5ab6-9e3a-9928ee1b850e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d114ac37-9962-5408-9d6e-05d942670183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347182} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df7bc06-d4ba-5a57-ab49-fe4b9ffa350a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b478a4e0-e373-5d4c-8f17-c97eaae0c7f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80136" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159c485c-001f-5031-b26e-f83f2bfeaf3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345538} {:answer/id #uuid "8be5f15a-55d3-55ce-9ad8-ff6f91eee7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346562} {:answer/id #uuid "936ec69f-b82c-51aa-b0f0-19d6eaccfe93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346640} {:answer/id #uuid "940a0cb2-9a0f-54ac-81e8-736d1cba6508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346643} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9d53ec-b344-5b9f-8c8e-a292d0704445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8be5f15a-55d3-55ce-9ad8-ff6f91eee7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346562} :answer-field/id #uuid "419a1eeb-4e9d-5a0c-8435-a16640782af5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9f0ee2e-ecf1-5ebf-8f05-484d1b3df712" :question/math-academy-id "q-80138" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b75e759-23b9-5a00-8a2f-7c0ca516d97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345736} {:answer/id #uuid "910cdafc-67fc-587c-87f0-bdba48190064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346616} {:answer/id #uuid "b75eed07-3b22-5842-aac1-37ec3c1762bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346950} {:answer/id #uuid "c106e615-06ca-5d4a-9046-a19b20c78b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347019} {:answer/id #uuid "d48896a5-f655-5550-962f-afc68a851fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910cdafc-67fc-587c-87f0-bdba48190064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346616} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b44f15-bb40-5519-8a0c-c8353afc8e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0f13e6-ba4b-56eb-b269-f07bb6ae2732" :question/math-academy-id "q-80303" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a7b19b50822235a2dcbfcdf74984735a1e26b2b775180b24bdc2bcbf24c588b.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- $x=1$ is **not** a simple root.\n- $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-2$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=-2$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433015c8-b02a-5d3b-9bc4-7270edca0b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345929} {:answer/id #uuid "444cadbf-3703-59f5-bad4-46404cc1a695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(x+1){(x-2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345940} {:answer/id #uuid "654ada61-08a2-5d34-998b-5c8db9d07e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346234} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbb7820-ea22-5b52-910b-269aa2f0dbbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}{(x+1)}^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346310} {:answer/id #uuid "f282e323-500b-5dcf-a254-2d713e281555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{(x+1)}^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654ada61-08a2-5d34-998b-5c8db9d07e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346234} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5873ca-3084-51bf-9b1d-3de807002492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad55c8cb-7248-5482-9f28-0afc365f8908" :question/math-academy-id "q-80348" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b086759a25af7e464c0c8be78d36cd4d8458c5c25e7c84cb6ecb28b2d59e9df.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-1$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b68fad9-861a-5a5d-b8d5-ff1e2fa15ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{7}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346430} {:answer/id #uuid "8724b35c-787a-52ef-80ff-eaf99f6a667a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{5}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346523} {:answer/id #uuid "94213796-5be7-57ae-81b2-17ed395cf18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{3}(x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346645} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ec07fb-d5b8-585e-82ea-e6c5d87d6e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}(x+2)^{5}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346910} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c1df98-76d9-50de-b003-8111fdce77bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}(x+2)(x-1)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94213796-5be7-57ae-81b2-17ed395cf18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(x+2)^{3}(x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346645} :answer-field/id #uuid "db472ae2-2f21-533d-93c0-3a75b3b10cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0154a31e-007f-558f-af24-913ca9813ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80352" :question/problem "![](images/19/1956e8c684cc03538877d3ec5d1e72d3d3f48b63341f7739b9a18962b080f1e3.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=-2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=1,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=1,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=-x(x+2)^{3}(x-1)^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01caf021-de3e-5e2a-89ce-ba289b65c0ff" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number in Exponential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d37a5d-f38e-5e9f-b4c2-8557a961d8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{17}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345385} {:answer/id #uuid "21e41ad5-4ee1-5fe7-b639-bc9b977dc4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{17}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.338\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345653} {:answer/id #uuid "2f556611-9499-54a3-9131-95ba2cbf06c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345767} {:answer/id #uuid "a3665dd9-b15a-5d76-8f3c-92d873804b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.338\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346766} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ae151a-4d90-50cb-ad3c-f2ecfc64dcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{4.989\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f556611-9499-54a3-9131-95ba2cbf06c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345767} :answer-field/id #uuid "e647b151-d969-5d70-9827-bf43ae96b615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a752e22-d98b-55fa-871d-a83c493259aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-8076" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=5-\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{5})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{5})| \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.197\\,39… \\\\  & ≈6.086.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{26}$ and $\\theta =6.086$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9be02ade-3dd2-5ec3-a532-78d1151dc89f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number Shown on an Argand Diagram in Exponential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd8fff4-79c1-5e30-9ce6-337b2a20b872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\,\\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349292} {:answer/id #uuid "79b311bf-65c7-58fc-b381-6bee661fc901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349569} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecd9ca2-c07f-5373-8dc5-c59dfe7aa36b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349609} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9d3205-d44e-57ec-95f3-fb33fbb6e446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.678\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349963} {:answer/id #uuid "f769ac7b-dfab-556e-af9c-c783920eee8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.678\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79b311bf-65c7-58fc-b381-6bee661fc901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349569} :answer-field/id #uuid "10320074-e8c3-5c1b-828c-3f9b61cfea74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92d2189e-bd4e-5b46-a533-1fb76600704b" :question/math-academy-id "q-8082" :question/problem "![](images/18/18dd1668d0e9eaeb1be7919821707bbf1d472b22b00acf68a921a3522b2f5f7f.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-4-2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-2}{-4})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})| \\\\  & ≈\\pi +0.463\\,64… \\\\  & ≈3.605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =3.605$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38cdd4-2914-5fbf-af5a-f896fca82487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348534} {:answer/id #uuid "46388f94-0b9a-5819-926f-46042d3247f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349085} {:answer/id #uuid "46e780ae-e427-5db3-9e7b-87fd979da562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349091} {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f16d9b-438b-5abd-801a-9c54b0392c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-4\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} :answer-field/id #uuid "07393c80-442e-58d3-986a-670d473a0e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebfe6ff0-a2a2-5246-ac85-4546ec479cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-81224" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cf5972-f40e-5217-8db4-af4d714d0f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,5)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348511} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff5e665-aaa0-5a32-9ee9-f1bfeace33e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349171} {:answer/id #uuid "86623a7d-5813-5d35-94af-16d6fb07a0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349673} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ecf398-d445-5f07-9e39-0e0872bbfc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350178} {:answer/id #uuid "e061e2c5-0620-5228-ad52-cedd9b116361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86623a7d-5813-5d35-94af-16d6fb07a0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349673} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a944731-6928-5c95-9932-5dd54d8c5f7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f524ac1c-6637-5a2f-a68c-7f9c181d3393" :question/math-academy-id "q-81297" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{5x-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-1 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd0aa5fc-79de-525e-a688-a8a2d2fcf858" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant in a Transformed Reciprocal Function Given the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8d2777-c68c-5bac-b02b-e76ea7832227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348585} {:answer/id #uuid "4440720a-4b25-56ac-acfb-e35454aaf42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349068} {:answer/id #uuid "aa03c041-3060-57a7-9832-43fc7a4091d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349958} {:answer/id #uuid "b607c09a-ead1-5f9f-8366-b91cefea5b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350056} {:answer/id #uuid "fe48bdf6-69b4-58e6-ab4b-56b33ca29990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa03c041-3060-57a7-9832-43fc7a4091d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349958} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ab50f6-ff51-5cc2-a2e4-1ac35c1df983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69d362d8-5140-5de9-97ea-ddf053c6a1b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81306" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-7}{kx-4}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{5})\\cup (\\frac{4}{5},\\infty ).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-4 & =0 \\\\ kx & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{4}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{4}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{k} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{k} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d82fa3-d576-5c42-9dca-4e1b76473424" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e75273e-f9fe-5904-9b97-df326138bc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348572} {:answer/id #uuid "56bde1c1-ec8e-5414-abd3-cf3a35ed829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349240} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec2a49-c0cb-50b3-96d0-78c9ed276005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349486} {:answer/id #uuid "710ec1a0-ca77-5baf-9aea-64447273a88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-6)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349487} {:answer/id #uuid "76603cce-9fe2-582e-98c8-b6b5dfe79841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,6)\\cup (6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ec2a49-c0cb-50b3-96d0-78c9ed276005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349486} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d01f1a9-302b-5e9b-a9b7-f275dfb86ddd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6996c90-1b1f-5226-b63d-1aa92068c6b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81416" :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-6}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=1.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6b1316dd9c7f2a40c23de08dde63cbc6402593c6209d99384279a7c1ae29da6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808a85c2-ca4a-542f-8886-908783750d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349626} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd3458d-3fcd-5615-89ef-119fa3a75163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349718} {:answer/id #uuid "99b11afc-eea4-51cc-90b6-2bcc44f1ccd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349822} {:answer/id #uuid "ec838bdb-4a1f-5b1a-b168-73687add9f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350513} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d92b5e-622a-5e98-8d37-c85026582415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cd3458d-3fcd-5615-89ef-119fa3a75163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349718} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6fff443-d103-5115-9f6d-56a670c1f10e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dc88ce5-1862-53d4-934e-e04fef66ce16" :question/math-academy-id "q-81532" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a08b123-1007-51d0-9626-6957cea1cbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348819} {:answer/id #uuid "57b185da-3c33-5e53-b896-4c58bbe65ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349253} {:answer/id #uuid "65cf0fb8-b80b-5dd5-873d-133d51e5ed5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +2\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349378} {:answer/id #uuid "d17b82e0-699f-5810-bbfd-d8426a2e29ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350286} {:answer/id #uuid "e029609d-c698-570a-9c3e-6c5b9a4f3d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b185da-3c33-5e53-b896-4c58bbe65ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349253} :answer-field/id #uuid "8380bcb3-1b34-51e5-86b8-2bf85dedf899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0891f6b-b8f3-59bf-bdab-23a45deeee45" :question/math-academy-id "q-81536" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\pi +2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Zeros of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ee5a37-5407-5f2c-b35d-818c7b51de99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348485} {:answer/id #uuid "a96ee961-091c-5bc7-94e9-4fc2414a6608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349955} {:answer/id #uuid "c965a8db-cf91-5534-9a2e-b2b2d81d2a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350220} {:answer/id #uuid "f934958a-918e-544c-9c79-ee9005be989f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350636} {:answer/id #uuid "ff59dfe4-69fc-5f06-a333-183188f57b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96ee961-091c-5bc7-94e9-4fc2414a6608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349955} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9569062-abe3-5fbf-aea5-b41339436a91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "483db40e-99c9-5ee4-bf30-82e490a0ea46" :question/math-academy-id "q-81542" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Zeros of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdbc89b-3531-5aca-8ba9-f97488020ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348987} {:answer/id #uuid "4113848c-c05d-566b-b1dc-5db84a4d0393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349038} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a3fae-b9d5-5b5e-bd0f-dcd4106b4c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349305} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb5e873-76c9-58bf-8d7a-7e75030cf3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349581} {:answer/id #uuid "abfe1148-d014-58cf-9667-7f0d1e092c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a3fae-b9d5-5b5e-bd0f-dcd4106b4c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6083aa-ab96-52a6-8f56-aa3dc31b367b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57be2016-4f15-558e-8c73-c2bdbd52fe97" :question/math-academy-id "q-81546" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 6x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{6},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3e445b-242f-5183-8134-e1a2873f586c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348570} {:answer/id #uuid "29c5d455-ea88-583b-878a-5b2da6e2eca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348815} {:answer/id #uuid "87848d38-9a7f-500f-8ee0-d03c8430c2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349681} {:answer/id #uuid "c66561f4-bc83-5fd5-927f-843045ce31e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350193} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d22d26-f36a-5676-9a92-d9439050a127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c5d455-ea88-583b-878a-5b2da6e2eca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348815} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d872cb-4071-5344-a1bc-bda53e5bcc36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7bdacc5-30cf-5786-b5bc-164c930bb79b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81567" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- If we add $4$ to the first interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\le -1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le -1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\le 3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If we add $4$ to the second interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 1+4 \\\\  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)+4\\ge 5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le 3,\\,f(x)\\ge 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$"}]}
